Daniel Kraft <d@domob.eu> wrote:
>
> Continuing on the "language to rewrite" war, I'd prefer C++, because
> this not only makes data structures easier with STL, but it also makes
> code cleaner as compared to C (at least it seems to me it's easier to
> do cleaner and better structured code).  I wouldn't want Java, because
> to me it is just a "poor C++" (at least if you make sensible use of
> C++ such that the "protection" Java offers against certain poor coding
> techniques does not  matter) and I'd only consider it for rare cases
> like when I want an Applet for a Webpage or a program that needs to
> run on multiple platforms *without recompiling*.

Shouldn't it be possible to start a C++ rewrite of NetHack with the
existing code base by changing only part of the code to C++?

I haven't done it in practice on a project with that much LOC but
Stroustroup justified a lot of the brain dead features of C++ with
this argument.

> But C would also be fine, if we adopt a good style; it seems there are
> really some people out there who are in favor of C.

In this case, the first thing to do would be to remove a lot of dead
code and cruft from the source files so that one could use a lot of
those nifty software engineering tools that have been invented since
1985. :-)

> Another idea
> (just because I recently worked with it) could be to do some mixed
> languages programming, like basic engine code in C/C++ and some of the
> NetHack-content atop of it using a scripting language like Guile for
> controlling the engine with the actual gameplay?

This by itself would be rather interesting to me as I have next to
none experience with how one does embed a scripting language into
another program.

